course: dictionary
lesson: 12842
title: Encompass: taking everything in
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Encompass is a formal verb. It means to include something, or to cover all of it. «The school project encompasses history and geography.» The project does not just touch on history and geography: it has both inside it, like a big box that holds them.
explain: Encompass can also mean to surround a place completely. «Thick darkness encompassed the cave.» The darkness was all around, on every side. Use the clues: a big box holds everything inside it, and a surround covers every side.
explain: Look for the feeling of "everything is inside". If you can swap in the words "includes" or "surrounds" and the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Encompass is more formal, so you will often find it in exams, school reports and descriptions in stories.
fact: Encompass is a verb: encompass, encompasses, encompassed, encompassing.
fact: «Encompass» means to include something, or to cover all of it.
fact: «Encompass» can also mean to surround a place completely.
fact: «Encompasses» means it includes or surrounds everything. Example: «The forest encompasses a lake and a hill.»
fact: Check a new word by swapping in a simpler one, such as includes or surrounds. If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
exercise: «The project encompasses history and geography.» What does encompasses mean here? | *Includes both history and geography | Forgets history and geography | Asks questions about history and geography | Argues with history and geography
exercise: «Darkness encompassed the cave.» Which word is closest in meaning to encompassed? | Dropped | *Surrounded | Lit | Ran
exercise: Which word could replace encompasses in «The forest encompasses a lake and a hill.»? | Shrinks | Escapes | *Includes | Empties
test: «The rules encompass everyone in the club.» What does encompass mean in this sentence? | Excludes, leaves out | Is pleased with | *Includes everyone, covers all of them | Breaks
test: Which sentence uses encompass correctly? | The bag encompasses my lunch and my books | *The new science topic encompasses forces, energy and light | The rain encompassed the sun | Her dog encompassed the ball, then ran off
exam: Which word best matches the meaning of encompass, to include all of something? | Reject | *Cover | Divide | Forget
